# Git Commands

Rules for handling git commands and other non-interactive commands in the depot repository.

<rule>
name: git_commands
description: Standards for handling git and other non-interactive commands
filters:
  - type: content
    pattern: "(?m)^\\s*(git|less|head|tail|more)\\s+"
  - type: content
    pattern: "(?m)^\\s*.*\\|\\s*(less|head|tail|more)\\s*$"

actions:
  - type: reject
    conditions:
      - pattern: "(?m)^\\s*(git|less|head|tail|more)\\s+(?!.*\\|\\s*cat)"
        message: "Commands that use pagers must pipe to 'cat' to avoid interactive prompts"
      - pattern: "(?m)^\\s*.*\\|\\s*(less|head|tail|more)\\s*$"
        message: "Use 'cat' instead of pagers in pipelines"

  - type: suggest
    message: |
      When using commands that may trigger pagers or interactive prompts:

      1. Always pipe to 'cat' for git commands that show output:
         ```bash
         # Bad: Will open in pager
         git log
         git diff
         git show

         # Good: Pipes to cat
         git log | cat
         git diff | cat
         git show | cat
         ```

      2. Use cat instead of less/more/head/tail:
         ```bash
         # Bad: Interactive pagers
         command | less
         command | more
         command | head
         command | tail

         # Good: Non-interactive alternatives
         command | cat
         command | cat | head -n 10
         command | cat | tail -n 10
         ```

      3. For git commands that don't show output (commit, push, etc), no pipe needed:
         ```bash
         git add .
         git commit -m "message"
         git push
         ```

      4. When needing specific line ranges, use cat with head/tail:
         ```bash
         # Show first 10 lines
         git log | cat | head -n 10

         # Show last 10 lines
         git log | cat | tail -n 10

         # Show lines 5-10
         git log | cat | head -n 10 | tail -n 6
         ```

examples:
  - input: |
      # Bad: Interactive pager
      git log
      git diff HEAD~1
      some_command | less

      # Good: Non-interactive output
      git log | cat
      git diff HEAD~1 | cat
      some_command | cat
    output: "Commands properly configured for non-interactive use"

metadata:
  priority: high
  version: 1.1
</rule>

